Description: A 3D project's resolution is one eye's in its renders
 A program's 3D options are "enable 3D, render resolution per eye, and a shortcut
 to Display Settings", and a program "never stretches or squeezes an eye". In a 3D
 project (its packing taken from its clips) the project's resolution is now that of
 one eye: a project of 1920x1080 renders 3840x1080, each eye a 1920x1080 picture.
 The render document's profile is doubled in width (its display aspect ratio with
 it), every clip's stereo3d_unpack filter fits its views to whole pictures
 (target_views=full), and a side by side clip with half views, which the project
 left as it was, gets the filter too. A project whose frame already holds two whole
 views (3840x1080) and a project whose packing the person declared by hand are
 written as before. With side by side marked, FFmpeg's Matroska muxer writes the
 DisplayWidth and DisplayHeight of one plane, which RFC 9559 section 18.10 asks for:
 "the DisplayWidth/DisplayHeight in pixels is the number of pixels for one plane
 (1920x1080 for that full HD stream)".
